WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.006 --> 00:00:05.604
As Macbeth left the chamber where the murdered king lay, he met Lady Macbeth in the hallway.

2
00:00:05.606 --> 00:00:11.002
She had been too anxious to sleep. "I have done the deed," he said.

3
00:00:11.003 --> 00:00:18.208
Then looking at her, he whispered, "Didn't you hear a noise? One of Duncan's servants . . . I thought he was awake.

4
00:00:18.209 --> 00:00:22.984
He mumbled, ‘God bless us,' as if he were praying in his sleep.

5
00:00:22.984 --> 00:00:26.764
I should have said, ‘Amen,' but the word stuck in my throat.

6
00:00:26.765 --> 00:00:37.518
I was so much in need of a blessing! Why couldn't I say ‘Amen'?" Then, looking at his bloody dagger, he whispered, "This is a sorry sight."

7
00:00:37.519 --> 00:00:46.716
"What are you doing with that?" Lady Macbeth gasped. "Go back! Leave the dagger by the servants, and cover them with blood."

8
00:00:46.717 --> 00:00:53.557
Macbeth shook his head. "I'll go no more. I don't dare look at what I've done."

9
00:00:53.558 --> 00:00:58.454
Without hesitating, Lady Macbeth took the dagger and hurried back to the king's room.

10
00:00:58.455 --> 00:01:06.208
When she returned, her hands were covered in blood. "Now my hands are the same color as yours," she said.

11
00:01:06.209 --> 00:01:10.740
"Quickly, let's go wash this filthy evidence from our hands."

12
00:01:10.741 --> 00:01:15.542
In the pale light of dawn, there was a loud knocking on the great gate of the castle.

13
00:01:15.543 --> 00:01:24.359
Macduff, who was the thane of Fife and one of Duncan's most trusted noblemen, had arrived to accompany the king on his journey that day.

14
00:01:24.360 --> 00:01:31.136
The gatekeeper, who had drunk too much wine the night before, rose from his post and rubbed his aching head.

15
00:01:31.137 --> 00:01:40.446
"The gatekeeper of hell couldn't be more miserable than me. In the name of the devil," he called, "who's there?"

16
00:01:40.447 --> 00:01:45.730
Macbeth, who had heard the knocking on the gate, arrived to welcome Macduff.

17
00:01:45.730 --> 00:01:51.584
As they walked to the king's chamber, Macbeth kept silent, but Macduff talked eagerly.

18
00:01:51.584 --> 00:01:55.289
"I can't remember such an evil night," he said.

19
00:01:55.290 --> 00:01:58.932
"At our camp, a cold wind kept blowing the fire out.

20
00:01:58.933 --> 00:02:03.686
After we fell asleep, our horses broke loose and ran wild with fear.

21
00:02:03.687 --> 00:02:10.247
I don't know what upset them, but the birds, too, it seemed, were flying madly and screeching all night."

22
00:02:10.248 --> 00:02:14.620
"Yes, it was a rough night," Macbeth said solemnly.

23
00:02:14.621 --> 00:02:19.453
They arrived at the king's chamber, and Macbeth let Macduff go in ahead of him.

24
00:02:19.454 --> 00:02:27.136
"O horror! Horror!" Macduff cried when he saw Duncan. "My tongue cannot speak the horror!"

25
00:02:27.137 --> 00:02:33.183
He left the room to wake the other noblemen, calling out, "Murder and treason!"

26
00:02:33.184 --> 00:02:38.621
So Macbeth stood alone, staring at the sleeping servants that his wife had smeared with blood.

27
00:02:38.622 --> 00:02:41.945
Outside the room, Banquo and Ross came running.

28
00:02:41.946 --> 00:02:47.302
Lady Macbeth came hurrying too, and Duncan's two sons were the last to arrive.

29
00:02:47.303 --> 00:02:51.183
"What is the matter?" asked Malcolm, the king's older son.

30
00:02:51.184 --> 00:02:55.496
"Your royal father has been murdered," said Macduff.

31
00:02:55.498 --> 00:02:59.097
"By who?" cried Donaldbain.

32
00:02:59.097 --> 00:03:02.894
"By the servants in his chamber," said Macduff.

33
00:03:02.894 --> 00:03:09.890
"Or so it seems. A bloody dagger lies between them and their hands and faces are covered in blood."

34
00:03:09.890 --> 00:03:14.995
While Macduff was describing the scene, Macbeth came out of the gruesome chamber.

35
00:03:14.996 --> 00:03:28.992
"Forgive my fury," he said, looking them all in the eye, "but anyone who loved the king and has a heart of courage would have done the same as I . . . I've killed the guilty servants!"

36
00:03:28.993 --> 00:03:34.651
News of the king's death spread quickly, and many anxious discussions were held throughout the castle.

37
00:03:34.652 --> 00:03:40.695
Not everyone believed that the two servants were to blame, while some thought that they had been paid to do it.

38
00:03:40.695 --> 00:03:46.073
Malcolm and Donaldbain feared that whoever killed their father would try to kill them next.

39
00:03:46.073 --> 00:03:52.357
They met secretly that night and decided to flee: Malcolm to England and Donaldbain to Ireland.

40
00:03:52.358 --> 00:03:57.445
When people learned that Duncan's sons had fled, many began to suspect them.

41
00:03:57.446 --> 00:04:04.588
After the funeral, with no one from Duncan's family left to take the throne, Macbeth was crowned king.

42
00:04:04.589 --> 00:04:12.366
Macbeth and Lady Macbeth moved to the king's palace at Forres, where they decided to hold a banquet for all their noblemen.

43
00:04:12.367 --> 00:04:18.231
At Macbeth's request, Banquo and his young son arrived early to meet with the new royal couple.

44
00:04:18.232 --> 00:04:25.564
Macbeth and Banquo talked casually, and Macbeth suggested to Banquo that he go for a ride with his son before dinner.

45
00:04:25.565 --> 00:04:29.421
As Banquo walked to the stables with his son, he felt anxious.

46
00:04:29.422 --> 00:04:36.485
"I worry that Macbeth got the crown through foul play," he thought, "and yet the witches foretold it all."

47
00:04:36.486 --> 00:04:38.056
He looked at his son.

48
00:04:38.057 --> 00:04:40.937
"Perhaps I will be the father of kings."

49
00:04:40.938 --> 00:04:46.866
Walking quickly through the palace, Macbeth was also thinking of the witches' prophecy about Banquo.

50
00:04:46.867 --> 00:04:55.326
He clenched his fists. "Why should I have to wear a fruitless crown? I can't sleep at night; I keep hearing voices.

51
00:04:55.327 --> 00:04:58.754
‘Macbeth shall sleep no more!' they say.

52
00:04:58.755 --> 00:05:05.937
‘Macbeth has murdered sleep!' Did I commit murder and corrupt my mind just so that Banquo's descendants could be kings?

53
00:05:05.938 --> 00:05:12.556
To be king is nothing, unless I am safely king!" He arrived at a small hidden door near the stables.

54
00:05:12.557 --> 00:05:15.444
Waiting for him there were two rough men.

55
00:05:15.445 --> 00:05:20.492
He whispered instructions to them, telling them where Banquo and his son would be riding.

56
00:05:20.493 --> 00:05:24.077
"Report back to me when they're dead," he said.

57
00:05:24.078 --> 00:05:30.125
The banquet hall was full that evening, and the tables were crowded when Macbeth and Lady Macbeth entered.

58
00:05:30.126 --> 00:05:37.402
As the guests rose from their seats to bow, she whispered to her husband, "Be bright and happy among our guests tonight.

59
00:05:37.403 --> 00:05:43.156
You spend too much time alone worrying. All your worries should have died with Duncan."

60
00:05:43.157 --> 00:05:48.165
"You don't understand," he said quietly as they walked to the head table.

61
00:05:48.166 --> 00:05:53.424
"My mind crawls with scorpions knowing that Banquo and his son are still alive.

62
00:05:53.431 --> 00:05:56.589
But I have arranged a dreadful deed."

63
00:05:56.591 --> 00:06:02.148
She looked at him questioningly, but all he said was, "You will applaud when it's done."

64
00:06:02.149 --> 00:06:07.354
During the banquet, Macbeth went from table to table, conversing with his guests.

65
00:06:07.355 --> 00:06:14.116
Many commented on Banquo's absence, and Macbeth replied that he missed his friend and hoped he would arrive soon.

66
00:06:14.117 --> 00:06:18.037
Suddenly, he noticed two men skulking at the doorway to the hall.

67
00:06:18.038 --> 00:06:26.807
He knew it was the assassins, returned from their task, and he went quickly to meet them. "There's blood on your face," he said.

68
00:06:26.808 --> 00:06:31.600
"It's Banquo's, then," answered the first murderer. "He's dead."

69
00:06:31.601 --> 00:06:41.450
"Good. And his son?" asked Macbeth. But Banquo's son had escaped, and when Macbeth heard this, he went into a rage and sent the murderers away.

70
00:06:41.451 --> 00:06:49.164
Lady Macbeth came out to get him. "My lord," she said, "your guests notice your absence."

71
00:06:49.165 --> 00:06:56.248
They returned to take their seats, but as they approached their chairs, Macbeth's face went white and he began to tremble.

72
00:06:56.249 --> 00:06:59.426
"What is it?" she asked. "Please, sit."

73
00:06:59.427 --> 00:07:08.370
Staring at his chair as if something unimaginably horrible were seated there, Macbeth yelled, "Don't look at me with your wounded and gory face!"

74
00:07:08.371 --> 00:07:12.521
The hall went silent. All the guests stopped and stared.

75
00:07:12.522 --> 00:07:21.751
"See there! Behold," Macbeth cried. "Can't you see! Look! He should be dead, but there he sits!"

76
00:07:21.759 --> 00:07:27.900
Hastily, Lady Macbeth turned to the guests. "Please, excuse my lord," she said.

77
00:07:27.901 --> 00:07:33.368
"He is not well. This is just a momentary fit and will soon pass."

78
00:07:33.369 --> 00:07:38.726
She grabbed her husband's arm. "Are you mad? You stare at nothing but a chair."

79
00:07:38.727 --> 00:07:49.226
"As sure as I am standing here, I saw him," Macbeth said. "With his dead eyes, and bleeding from his gaping wounds, it was Banquo's ghost!"

